# D. Counterexample attack

## D.1. Branching compensation fails without restrictions

Consider the double star with adjacent branching vertices u,vu,vu,v, two leaves attached to each.

For an arbitrary two-round burning attempt, the maximum coverage is five of the six vertices. A maximum-coverage sequence starts at one branching vertex, say uuu, and places the second source on a leaf attached to vvv.

The final unburned component is the other leaf of vvv, whose boundary vertex has degree 3.

No maximum-coverage two-round sequence moves this residual component to a boundary of degree at most 2.

The tree has

n2=0.n_2=0.n2=0.

So the unrestricted idea that branching boundaries can always be removed, or that every persistence layer must consume degree-2 vertices, is false.

Qualification: for this tree the actual square parameter is

⌈6⌉=3.\lceil\sqrt6\rceil=3.⌈6⌉=3.

It is not a counterexample to the target. It only refutes the unrestricted intermediate mechanism.

## D.2. A single long path can be reused temporally

A path can have the same unburned interval surviving several rounds. The fact that it survives several times does not produce several disjoint sets of degree-2 vertices.

This is precisely where a temporal charging argument can double-count.

A proof must exhibit an injection from persistence events into fresh degree-2 vertices, or into disjoint path segments. Merely associating one path with each round is insufficient.

