# H. Exact remaining gap

The Critical Active-Thread Lemma is strong enough to imply the target, but the definition of an active thread alone does not imply the required load inequality.

The single hardest missing statement is:

If a minimum-order actual-q counterexample exists,then a lexicographically maximal failed q-round sequencemust leave residual vertices on threads carrying totaldegree-2 subdivision load at least 2q−1.\boxed{ \begin{gathered} \text{If a minimum-order actual-}q\text{ counterexample exists,}\\ \text{then a lexicographically maximal failed }q\text{-round sequence}\\ \text{must leave residual vertices on threads carrying total}\\ \text{degree-2 subdivision load at least }2q-1. \end{gathered} }If a minimum-order actual-q counterexample exists,then a lexicographically maximal failed q-round sequencemust leave residual vertices on threads carrying totaldegree-2 subdivision load at least 2q−1.

The available minimality argument says that, except at the lower endpoint of a square block, every degree-2 vertex is individually critical: suppressing it lowers the burning number from q+1q+1q+1 to qqq.

But individual criticality does not show that different temporal obstructions use different vertices.

That is the decisive gap.

A complete argument needs to establish a non-overlapping extraction principle: each relevant failure of the qqq-round covering must be associated with subdivision material that cannot be reused to account for another failure. The current candidate does not yet supply that principle.

The most useful next step is therefore not to count rounds, but to define and prove an exchange or packing theorem for the terminal-active threads of a maximal failed burning sequence. If that theorem fails even for a minimum actual-qqq counterexample, the corridor-charging strategy should be abandoned in favor of a different invariant.

